course: dictionary
lesson: 2087
title: Contact: touching, talking and getting in touch
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Contact is a word with several meanings, and the sentence around it tells you which one. Two things in contact are touching each other. Contact can also mean talking to someone. It is a noun and a verb: you can have contact with someone, or you can contact them.
explain: «Contact with nature and many kinds of microbes may help a child's immune system develop.» Here contact means touching or being near. «A child stays home alone only if... the child knows how to contact them.» Here contact is a verb meaning call, write or message. Ask yourself: is something touching, or is someone being reached?
explain: A contact can also be a person you can get in touch with, or their details. Phrases help too: «in contact with» means touching or in touch, «lose contact» means stop being in touch, and «eye contact» means looking directly at someone.
fact: Contact means touching: two things in contact are touching each other.
fact: Contact means communication with someone, by meeting, phone, letter or message.
fact: To contact someone is to call, write or message them.
fact: A contact is a person you know and can get in touch with, or their details.
fact: In an emergency, contact emergency services: 999 in the UK and Ireland, 911 in the USA and Canada, 000 in Australia, 111 in New Zealand.
exercise: «Contact with nature may help a child's immune system develop.» What does contact mean here? | A small lens worn on the eye | *Touching or being near | Losing touch with a friend | Phoning someone to chat
exercise: What is a contact? | A sport played on a muddy pitch | *A person you know and can get in touch with | A kind of gunpowder | A bright light used in a science lab
exercise: Which emergency number is 911? | Australia | *The USA and Canada | New Zealand | The UK and Ireland
test: «She lost contact with her old school friends.» What does lost contact mean? | Lost her glasses | Found a new bag | *Stopped being in touch | Touched them by mistake
test: Pushing a box across the floor is a ___ force, because your hand touches it. | non-contact | magnetism | *contact | gravity
exam: Which sentence uses contact as a verb? | Her eye contact made me smile | The two wires are in contact | *Please contact the school if you are ill | Keep your contact details safe
